<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinavar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dinawar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dinavar was a medieval city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a>, whose ruins lie near the modern village of the same name. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> it was a provincial centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> and the home of scholars such as the polymath al-Dinawari. In the 10th and 11th centuries it was held by the Kurdish Hasanwayhid and Annazid dynasties.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dinavar lay in the centre of the ancient region of Media. Its position made it the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> and a crossroads between the culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and that of the peoples on the other side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> its district bordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulwan">Hulwan</a> to the west, Masabadhan to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> to the east and Adharbayjan to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town is first attested as a foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, though it may be older, and like neighbouring Kangavar it had a Greek population. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> it was an important fortified place, reportedly attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> early in the 6th century. It was conquered in 642, after the Sasanian defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a>. Under the Umayyad caliph Mu'awiya I it was renamed Mah al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and made one of the two districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>, the other being Karmisin (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a>).</p><p>Dinavar flourished under the Umayyad and Abbasid caliphates; in the 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> described it as only a third smaller than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>. It produced scholars such as Ibn Qutaybah (died 889), Fakhr-un-Nisa and Abu Hanifa Dinawari (died 896), whose Kitāb al-akhbār al-ṭiwāl is an early effort to combine Iranian and Islamic history. It was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a> in 931 and, according to Ibn Athir, plundered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> of the Iva tribe in 1172/73.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 14th-century geographer Hamdallah Mustawfi knew Dinavar as a small town in his Nuzhat al-Qulub, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> destroyed it by the end of that century. Only a field of ruins remains, in Dinavar District of Sahneh County, Kermanshah Province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dinavar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abu'l-Shawk Faris extended their power to <b>Dinavar</b> and Kermanshah, but family feuds were frequent.</blockquote><a href="/state/annazid-dynasty">Annazid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Hasanwayh ibn Husayn, a chief of the Kurdish Barzikani tribe, took control of the mountains around <b>Dinavar</b>, Hamadan, Nahavand and Shahrizor in 959, as Buyid power was still being consolidated.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasanwayhid-dynasty">Hasanwayhid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
